## 1. What is a Corpus? & A Brief History

### Formal Definition
According to McEnery, Xiao, and Tono, a **corpus** is a collection of **electronic**, **naturally occurring** texts (written or spoken) which are **selected** to be **representative** of a particular language or language variety[cite: 10]:
* **Electronic:** Available in digital format so computers can process it with specialized software[cite: 10].
* **Naturally Occurring:** Real-world written or spoken language rather than experimental data[cite: 10].
* **Selected (Sampled):** Sampled systematically from the vast amount of language in use[cite: 10].
* **Representative:** Structured to reflect the general use of a target language or variety[cite: 10].

### Historical Timeline
* **Pre-Computational Concordances:** In medieval and early modern times, concordances were compiled by hand[cite: 12]. Alexander Cruden’s *Concordance of the Holy Scriptures* indexed every word of the Bible and took one year of work at 18 hours a day[cite: 12].
* **1970 & The Birth of Modern Corpus Linguistics:** Six years after Lancaster University was founded (with Bailrigg House standing as its oldest campus building), computer technology became prevalent across universities and institutions[cite: 10]. In *The Times* newspaper in 1970, the word *computer* (singular and plural) appeared **6,323 times**—more than twice as often as new UK Prime Minister Edward Heath (>3,000 mentions across ~300 articles) and over seven times more than the Apollo 13 space program (833 mentions across 264 articles)[cite: 10].
* **The LOB Corpus (1970–1978):** In 1970 at Lancaster, Geoffrey Leech began collecting a 1-million-word dataset of written British English modeled on the **Brown Corpus** (compiled at Brown University in Providence, Rhode Island)[cite: 9, 10]. After nearly a decade of negotiating copyright for ~500 texts with primitive computing resources, collaboration with Stig Johansson in Oslo and colleagues in Bergen produced the **Lancaster-Oslo/Bergen (LOB) Corpus** in 1978[cite: 10].
* **Major English Corpora Milestones:**
  * **British English:** London-Lund Corpus, COBUILD Corpora, British National Corpus (BNC 1994), and BNC 2014[cite: 10, 15].
  * **American English:** American National Corpus (ANC) and Corpus of Contemporary American English (COCA)[cite: 10, 15].
  * **International & Historical:** International Corpus of English (ICE), Wellington Corpus of New Zealand English, and *The Times* Corpus (all issues since 1785; the 1970 subcorpus alone approaches 500 million words)[cite: 10].

