## 2. Core Lenses: Concordance & Frequency

> *"If asked what is one benefit that corpora can provide and that cannot be provided by other means, I would reply 'information about frequency.'"* — **Geoffrey Leech**[cite: 13]

Concordance and frequency serve as two primary lenses for observing linguistic data: **concordance reveals a word's meaning (usage in context)**, and **frequency reveals a word's importance**[cite: 12].

### Concordances (KWIC)
A **concordance** is a collection of occurrences of a search term—called the **Node** or **Keyword in Context (KWIC)**—displayed with its surrounding **Left (L)** and **Right (R)** context[cite: 12, 16, 17].

### Frequency Metrics
Always report frequency using **both** absolute and relative values[cite: 13]:

| Metric | Definition | Analytical Value |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Absolute (Raw) Frequency** | The total number of hits/occurrences of a word or phrase in a corpus[cite: 13]. | Shows exact sample size of observed instances[cite: 13]. |
| **Relative (Normalized) Frequency** | Frequency per a standard number of words (typically **per 1 million words**), shown in parentheses in `#LancsBox X`[cite: 13, 17]. | Estimates probability of occurrence and allows comparison across corpora or genres of different sizes[cite: 13, 17]. |
| **Dispersion / Distribution** | How an expression is spread across different texts, genres, and registers[cite: 12, 17]. | Indicates whether a term is broadly used or concentrated in a few texts[cite: 12, 17]. |

#### Relative Frequency Equation
```text
Relative Frequency = (Absolute Frequency / Total Running Words [Tokens]) × Normalization Basis (1,000,000)
```
[cite: 13]

### Zipf's Law
Detailed in *Statistics in Corpus Linguistics: A Practical Guide* (2018), **Zipf's Law** states that regardless of corpus size, the frequency of a word is inversely proportional to its rank in a frequency list[cite: 13]:
* The **1st** most frequent word (`the` in English, ~6 million hits in a 100M-word corpus) has twice the frequency of the **2nd** (`of`, ~3 million hits), three times the frequency of the **3rd** (`and`), and so on[cite: 13].
* Grammatical words occupy the top ranks, followed by high-frequency content words (*people, time, year*), before trailing off into a long tail of mid- and low-frequency words[cite: 13].
* **Takeaway:** Words are rare; large corpora (millions or billions of words) are necessary to observe meaningful patterns[cite: 13].

